On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:33:01 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
> I am curious about this... the previous version used the patch file,
> which I think is the right way to do this. Why did you move away from
> that approach for this round?

I made a mistake. Your v6 review objected to the post-fetch block, and
I followed security/nebula: fetch the newer sonic with GH_TUPLE and
swap it into vendor/ in pre-patch. I then pinned the tuple to the
unreleased commit that carries the mmap fix, since it also contains
v1.15.4. That made PATCHFILES redundant, so I dropped it. Nobody asked
for that, and it undid what you asked for in your v3 review.

> The post-fetch and post-extract were what I object to. If it's possible
> to use the defined version, and apply the one mmap patch, I think that
> would be ideal.

v8 [1] does that with the closest version that builds. The tuple is
now the sonic v1.15.4 release tag, and the mmap fix comes back as
PATCHFILES from upstream commit bf12ab8, applied to the tuple with
PATCH_WRKSRC=${WRKSRC_sonic}. There is still no post-fetch and no
post-extract. The upstream patch touches only loader/mmap_unix.go and
applies cleanly to v1.15.4. The port builds, stages and passes
check-plist offline, and the binary runs.

> If that's not possible, fine, but I want to understand why.

The version upstream pins, v1.14.1, does not compile with any Go in
the tree. Its internal/rt defines GoMapIterator only for go1.24 &&
!go1.26, and Mk/Uses/go.mk offers only 1.26 and 1.27. Keeping v1.14.1
would mean also backporting sonic's Go 1.26 support, which is far more
than one patch. v1.15.4 is the newest sonic release, and it needs no
module that v1.14.1 did not.

>> +%%DATADIR%%/example.yaml
>
> Does example.yaml match the defaults, and so can be installed to
> /usr/local/etc/signoz.yaml? Research packages from other sources
> (probably linux package repos for the most part) to get ideas for how to
> package this.

No to both. I compared it key by key with the defaults in the Go
code, and it is a sample, not the defaults. Installed as it stands,
signoz exits before startup on three placeholder values:
sharder.single.org_id "org_id" is not a UUID, user.root.email "" is
not an email, and global.external_url "<unset>" is not a valid URL.
With those fixed it still changes behavior. Alert grouping becomes
1m/1m/1h instead of 30s/5m/4h, span metrics are switched on, password
self-reset is allowed, the cache halves, and the cloud agent is
pinned to an older version. Its own header says "Do not modify this
file".

There is no other packaging to borrow from. AUR, nixpkgs, Homebrew
and Repology have no signoz. Upstream's Docker image runs from /root
with no config file. Its systemd example in SigNoz/foundry unpacks the
release tarball into /opt/signoz and sets every path through SIGNOZ_*
environment variables, never reading example.yaml.

v8 therefore installs a short /usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml as an
@sample. It sets only the ClickHouse DSN, which is the setting
everyone changes, and points to example.yaml as the reference to every
key. example.yaml stays in DATADIR as that reference.

>> +%%DATADIR%%/templates/alertmanager/email.gotmpl
>> +%%DATADIR%%/templates/email/invitation.gotmpl
>> +%%DATADIR%%/templates/email/reset_password.gotmpl
>
> Tell me more about these files. If they might be used in modified form
> in the config, then do we need a /usr/local/etc/signoz dir, potentially
> using /usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml for the config, so that we can
> include additional configs?

They are the HTML bodies of the mail signoz sends. The two email
templates are the user invitation and the password reset. They are
read from emailing.templates.directory, which must hold both files;
if one is missing, signoz logs a warning and that email fails to send.
alertmanager/email.gotmpl defines the layout of alert notification
email, and alertmanager.signoz.templates is a list of globs, so a
later file can redefine it. None of them is embedded in the binary.

So yes, they are configuration that users will want to edit, and v8
installs all three as @sample files in
/usr/local/etc/signoz/templates, as sysutils/munin-master does with
its templates, so edits survive upgrades. The compiled-in defaults
point there.

signoz has no default config path. It takes --config any number of
times, merges the files in order key by key, then applies SIGNOZ_*
variables. In v8, rc.d defaults signoz_config to
/usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml and accepts a space-separated list,
so additional configs sit beside it, e.g.

    sysrc signoz_config="/usr/local/etc/signoz/signoz.yaml \
        /usr/local/etc/signoz/local.yaml"

signoz_config="" still runs on built-in defaults and signoz_env.

